did you know that your brain sees more  than you do let me explain in order for  us to understand and interact with the  world around us our brain must process  or make sense of what the eye see  information travels from the eyes  through the brain getting processed  along the way the first most basic stage  of processing occurs at a region called  the primary visual cortex afterward the  processing becomes increasingly complex  it divides into two streams with  distinct functions the ventral stream  flows along the lower region of the  brain it is often called the what  pathway because processing in these  regions allows to identify what we see  the dorsal stream flows along the top  region of the brain it is called the  where or how pathway because it helps us  to identify where objects are and how to  interact with them the dorsal stream  allows us to use vision to make precise  effective movements one of the ways we  know about these two separate streams is  because of evidence from patients with  very specific brain damage sometimes  people can sustain damage to one visual  stream but not the other one famous  patient known as DF lost function of her  what pathway in the ventral stream but  was still able to interact with objects  using the how pathway in the dorsal  stream for example she was not able to  describe the orientation of a slot she  was clearly able to see but was able to  reach out and insert a card into the  slot so even though she couldn't  perceive the orientation her brain could  use the information to guide action this  condition is known as visual agnosia the  reverse is called optic ataxia and it's  more rare but can also occur damage to  the hull pathway in the dorsal stream  but not the what pathway in the ventral  stream means that optic ataxia patients  can't use objects correctly but tends to  perceive what they are  researchers have also shown the same  separation between perception and action  in healthy participants by using visual  illusions that make objects seem larger  or smaller than they actually are take a  look at this image when we look at the  circles the illusion tricks are ventral  stream into perceiving this one as  larger than this one even though they're  actually the same size but when we reach  out to pick up a circle even if we  perceive it as larger than it is we are  able to grasp it based on its actual  size thanks to the dorsal stream  essentially the illusion fooled the  ventral stream but not the dorsal stream  this separation between perception and  action in patients and healthy  participants shows that the two visual  processing streams and their functions  are independent that being said in  everyday life it's rare you'd ever  notice the difference between vision for  perception and vision for action the  dorsal and ventral streams work together  to help us perform daily tasks and  researchers are continuing to study the  exact relationship between the two so  even though your brain sees more than  you do it's very good at integrating all  of your vision into one seamless  experience of the world and allowing you  to interact with it  [Music]
 

